<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Taymiyya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1263">1263</a>–<a href="/years/1328">1328</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ibn Taymiyyah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Taymiyya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim scholar, jurist and theologian of the Hanbali school, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 1263, whose family fled the Mongol invasion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1269. He issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> declaring the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a> unbelievers and calling for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against them, fought at the Battle of Marj al-Saffar, and wrote polemics against Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asharism">Ash'arism</a> and philosophy and against the veneration of saints at their tombs. Accused of anthropomorphism and censured for his rulings, he was imprisoned several times and died in the Citadel of Damascus in 1328. His writings later influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> and modern Salafi and militant Islamist movements.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ibn Taymiyya</b> ruled that they should be killed and their women enslaved, and the Ottomans often relied on his ruling.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Islamism had forerunners: the jurist <b>Ibn Taymiyyah</b> in the 13th and 14th centuries, Ahmad Sirhindi in India, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab, the founder of Wahhabism, Shah Waliullah, and the Deobandi movement founded around 1867.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The ulama owed their posts to the sultan, who could bend them: al-Nasir Muhammad compelled the qadi to rule in his interest, and <b>Ibn Taymiyya</b>, the most prominent scholar of the age, was arrested several times for his teachings.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
